  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nInitial trough will continue east across the area, taking the \nmeasurable precipitation with it. Decent CAA will take place in its \nwake, with 850mb temps dropping from the teens to +2 to +4 tonight. \nCloud cover will limit overall impacts of this, but overnight lows \nwill still be 10 to 15 degrees cooler than last night (mid to upper \n40). \n\nFocus thereafter will be on the next, stronger trough which should \nbe located from NE WI into NE IA at 12Z Thu. It will be a slow push \nSE, reaching our NW counties closer to 22 or 23Z and then \nprogressing across the area during the evening. Models vary on \namount of precip with this feature but it would seem reasonable \ngiven the amount of forcing and lingering low level moisture to see \nsome sprinkles or light showers at times in the afternoon then maybe \nsomewhat better coverage near/along the front. In its wake, even \ncolder air arrives with 850 mb temps dropping below zero by 12Z Fri \n(coldest NW where values of -2 to -4 C will occur). This, combined \nwith lake temps in the mid teens will yield sufficient delta ts to \ngenerate a lake response towards 12Z Fri. Ptype still appears to be \nall rain (albeit cold), but with air temps Fri AM being near 40s a \nheavier band could tap sufficient air to produce some graupel or a \nfew snow flakes. The worst of the coverage will likely occur between \n12 and 18Z with some slow decrease towards 00Z and ending thereafter. \nHighs Friday and Sat will struggle to reach 50 (a hair below normal \nfor early Nov). Overnight lows will dip into the lower 30s with a \nfreeze possible in some areas Saturday night as winds diminish and \nskies clear. Growing season is over, so no headlines will be issued. \n\nPattern calms down with a slow moderating trend in temps next week \nto near or slightly above normal levels. Med range models are \nstruggling with a southern stream wave and its impacts on moisture \nreturn ahead of the next trough mid week. Dry forecast is maintained \nat this time.",
